      interactions: ["Rimegepant and ubrogepant exposure can rise with CYP3A or P-gp inhibition and fall enough to lose efficacy with CYP3A induction. Atogepant dosing changes with CYP3A inhibitors or inducers and OATP inhibitors. Zavegepant should be avoided with OATP1B3 or NTCP inhibitors or inducers, and an intranasal decongestant can reduce nasal absorption.", "An interaction matters because it can either raise exposure and adverse effects or lower exposure below an effective level; the remedy may be avoidance, dose reduction, or a longer interval depending on the exact product."],
      nursingEssentials: ["First identify intent: attack treatment or prevention, oral ODT/tablet or nasal spray, and exact product. Then verify the maximum daily dose, any monthly exposure boundary, hepatic and renal suitability, pregnancy, blood pressure, digital perfusion, and every CYP3A/P-gp/OATP/NTCP interaction.", "When a label says safety beyond a number of treated attacks or doses is not established, that is an evidence boundary rather than a magic toxicity threshold. Exceeding it means the regimen has moved outside adequately studied exposure and should trigger reassessment of migraine control, medication-overuse patterns, preventive therapy, and the diagnosis.", "Investigate a sudden worst headache, focal deficit, fever or meningismus, pregnancy-related red flags, or a major pattern change instead of automatically treating it as the patient's usual migraine."],
