      mechanism: "Trigeminal sensory activation releases CGRP, which normally activates the CLR/RAMP1 receptor complex, raises cAMP, and amplifies nociceptive and vasodilatory signaling. Erenumab prevents receptor activation; the ligand antibodies bind CGRP before it can activate the receptor. Because antibodies are catabolized into peptides and amino acids rather than cleared through CYP pathways, conventional CYP interactions are unlikely. Their approximately 27- to 31-day effective half-lives support monthly or quarterly dosing but also explain why benefit and adverse effects fade slowly after a dose is stopped.",
